{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2160 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "a8b626b94749fade1f7e5a06c5e415df512d4195cb7294f48357c2ca06fc3dd8" :edb/db-before-t 2159 :edb/db-after-t 2160 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535472 50 #inst "2026-10-14T04:22:32.294Z" 13194139535472 17592186045443 true] [17592186134111 1074 "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1.2 \\\\ 2.5 \\\\ 3.6 \\\\ 4.5\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}15 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}11.8 \\\\ 35\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 \\\\ 1.1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=0.2+1.1x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the  height after $8$ years, we substitute $x=8$ into the equation of the line: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.2+1.1(8) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, according to the model, the height of a pine tree that is $8$ years old will be $9$ feet. \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=8$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable." 13194139535472 17592186045443 true] [17592186134111 1074 "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1.2 \\\\ 2.5 \\\\ 3.6 \\\\ 4.5\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}15 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}11.8 \\\\ 35\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.2 \\\\ 1.1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=0.2+1.1x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the  height after $8$ years, we substitute $x=8$ into the equation of the line: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.2+1.1(8) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, according to the model, the height of a pine tree that is $8$ years old will be $9$ feet. \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=8$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable." 13194139535472 17592186045443 false] [17592186134187 1074 "We need to find a matrix $E$ such that $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{3}$ to $A.$\n\nTo find $E,$ we apply the given row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication is $EA:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot A\n$$" 13194139535472 17592186045443 false] [17592186134187 1074 "We need to find a matrix $E$ such that $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{3}$ to $A.$\n\nTo find $E,$ we apply the given row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication is $EA:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$" 13194139535472 17592186045443 true] [17592186134189 1071 "Consider $E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{2}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A.$ Find the row operation that, when applied to $A,$ is equivalent to multiplying $A$ by the given elementary matrix $E$ from the left." 13194139535472 17592186045443 false] [17592186134189 1071 "Consider $E=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{2}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A.$ Find the row operation that, when applied to $A,$ is equivalent to multiplying $A$ by the given elementary matrix $E$ from the left." 13194139535472 17592186045443 true] [17592186134189 1074 "The given matrix $E$ is obtained by multiplying the second row of ${I}_{3}$ by $-\\frac{2}{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{2}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary \nrow operation ${R}_{2}:=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2}$ to $A.$" 13194139535472 17592186045443 false] [17592186134189 1074 "The given matrix $E$ is obtained by multiplying the second row of ${I}_{3}$ by $-\\frac{2}{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{2}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary \nrow operation ${R}_{2}:=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2}$ to $A.$" 13194139535472 17592186045443 true] [17592186134190 1071 "Consider $E=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A.$\nFind the row operation that, when applied to $A,$ is equivalent to multiplying $A$ by the given elementary matrix $E$ from the left." 13194139535472 17592186045443 false] [17592186134190 1071 "Consider $E=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A.$\nFind the row operation that, when applied to $A,$ is equivalent to multiplying $A$ by the given elementary matrix $E$ from the left." 13194139535472 17592186045443 true] [17592186134190 1074 "The given matrix $E$ is obtained by swapping the first and the second rows of ${I}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary \nrow operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}$ to $A.$" 13194139535472 17592186045443 false] [17592186134190 1074 "The given matrix $E$ is obtained by swapping the first and the second rows of ${I}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary \nrow operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}$ to $A.$" 13194139535472 17592186045443 true] [17592186134201 1071 "If $u=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,,$ $v=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,,$ and $u\\cdot u=15,$ then $\\frac{u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}u)}{u\\cdot (\\frac{3}{4}v)}=$" 13194139535472 17592186045443 false] [17592186134201 1071 "If $u=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,,$ $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,,$ and $u\\cdot u=15,$ then $\\frac{u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}u)}{u\\cdot (\\frac{3}{4}v)}=$" 13194139535472 17592186045443 true] [17592186134203 1071 "Given $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$, find $‖x‖.$" 13194139535472 17592186045443 false] [17592186134203 1071 "Given $x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$, find $‖x‖.$" 13194139535472 17592186045443 true] [17592186134206 1071 "Given $a=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$, find $‖a‖.$" 13194139535472 17592186045443 false] [17592186134206 1071 "Given $a=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$, find $‖a‖.$" 13194139535472 17592186045443 true] [17592186134207 1071 "Normalize the vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535472 17592186045443 false] [17592186134207 1071 "Normalize the vector $x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$" 13194139535472 17592186045443 true] [17592186134207 1074 "To normalize a vector $x,$ we have to divide the vector by its norm: $\\frac{x}{‖x‖}.$ Therefore, to normalize $x,$ we first calculate $‖x‖$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖x‖ & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}+{x}_{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+1+4+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide $x$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{‖x‖} & =\\frac{x}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{-1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{-2}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535472 17592186045443 false] [17592186134207 1074 "To normalize a vector $x,$ we have to divide the vector by its norm: $\\frac{x}{‖x‖}.$ Therefore, to normalize $x,$ we first calculate $‖x‖$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖x‖ & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}+{x}_{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+1+4+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide $x$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{‖x‖} & =\\frac{x}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{-1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{-2}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535472 17592186045443 true] [17592186134209 1071 "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u\\,⊥\\,v.$ Find $(v+w)\\cdot u+v\\cdot (u+w).$" 13194139535472 17592186045443 false] [17592186134209 1071 "Let $u=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $w=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ be vectors such that $u\\,⊥\\,v.$ Find $(v+w)\\cdot u+v\\cdot (u+w).$" 13194139535472 17592186045443 true] [17592186134211 1071 "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 3 \\\\ b \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u$ is orthogonal to $v$ and $w.$ What is the value of $a+b?$" 13194139535472 17592186045443 false] [17592186134211 1071 "Let $u=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 3 \\\\ b \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix},v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},$ and $w=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ be vectors such that $u$ is orthogonal to $v$ and $w.$ What is the value of $a+b?$" 13194139535472 17592186045443 true] [17592186134215 1071 "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n- **U.** $U={[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]}$\n- **V.** $V={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}28 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]}$\n- **W.** $W={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139535472 17592186045443 false] [17592186134215 1071 "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n- **U.** $U={\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}}$\n- **V.** $V={\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}28 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}}$\n- **W.** $W={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139535472 17592186045443 true] [17592186134215 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2}},$ \n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0 \\\\ {v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}=1 \\\\ {v}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{3}{10}+\\frac{3}{10}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{9}{10}+\\frac{1}{10}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{9}{10}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned