4 "First, we need to find a basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ is a basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nFinally, we find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot 1}{{(-1)}^{2}+{1}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{-8}{2}b \\\\  & =-4b \\\\  & =-4\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535472 17592186045443 true] [17592186134295 1071 "Find the acute angle between the vectors $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535472 17592186045443 false] [17592186134295 1071 "Find the acute angle between the vectors $v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $w=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}.$" 13194139535472 17592186045443 true] [17592186134296 1071 "Find the vector in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ \nthat is closest to the vector $x,$ where \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535472 17592186045443 false] [17592186134296 1071 "Find the vector in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ \nthat is closest to the vector $x,$ where \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139535472 17592186045443 true] [17592186134296 1074 "First, notice that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe closest vector to $x$ in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace, which is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 12\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535472 17592186045443 false] [17592186134296 1074 "First, notice that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe closest vector to $x$ in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace, which is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 12\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535472 17592186045443 true] [17592186134300 1071 "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\},$ where\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{v,w\\}$ is orthogonal." 13194139535472 17592186045443 false] [17592186134300 1071 "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\},$ w