 has the standard matrix \n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 8 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat are the respective images of the standard basis vectors $\\{i,j,k\\}$ of ${R}^{3}$ under the action of $T?$" 13194139535472 17592186045443 true] [17592186134428 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nThe images of the vectors in the standard basis $\\{i,j,k\\}$ are the first, second, and third columns of $T,$ respectively. \n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =T[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T(j) & =T[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T(k) & =T[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the images of the vectors in the standard basis $\\{i,j,k\\}$ by computing the matrix products $T\\cdot i,$ $T\\cdot j,$ and $T\\cdot k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =T\\cdot i=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 8 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T(j) & =T\\cdot j=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 8 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T(k) & =T\\cdot k=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 8 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535472 17592186045443 false] [17592186134428 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nThe images of the vectors in the standard basis $\\{i,j,k\\}$ are the first, second, and third columns of $T,$ respectively. \n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =T\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(j) & =T\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(k) & =T\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the images of the vectors in the standard basis $\\{i,j,k\\}$ by computing the matrix products $T\\cdot i,$ $T\\cdot j,$ and $T\\cdot k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =T\\cdot i=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 8 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(j) & =T\\cdot j=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 8 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(k) & =T\\cdot k=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 8 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535472 17592186045443 true] [17592186134429 1074 "Every linear transformation in ${R}^{2}$ can be represented as a $2\\times 2$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $i$ and $j$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for the linear transformation is\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535472 17592186045443 false] [17592186134429 1074 "Every linear transformation in ${R}^{2}$ can be represented as a $2\\times 2$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $i$ and $j$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for the linear transformation is\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139535472 17592186045443 true] [17592186134430 1071 "The linear transformation $T$ has the standard matrix \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -4 \\