 image of $S_1,$ it can be parametrized by varying $u$ only. Similarly, since $S_2'$ is the image of $S_2,$ it can be parametrized by varying $v$ only. So, the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively, are given by\n\n![](images/1b/1be386cd85ad48bcc75b4d292ebeeee0d8af0bf8df2297696b9384c448130bb3.svg)\n\nNow, since $\\delta u$ is small, $S_1'$ can be approximated by a line segment. Similarly, since $\\delta v$ is small, $S_2'$ can also be approximated by a line segment. Let's construct two vectors $\\mathbf{s}_1$ and $\\mathbf{s}_2$ that span our two segments, as shown below.\n\n![](images/1f/1ff10cdcde237698a28c6f76053de50745ef12c67dfc5290ac6e06893d201972.png)\n\nWe can find the vector $\\mathbf s_1$ by subtracting the position vectors of its endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{1}=a-[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{0} \\\\ {y}_{0}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}x\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0}) \\\\ y\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}x\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0}) \\\\ y\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}x\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})-x\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0}) \\\\ y\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})-y\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0})\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that when $\\delta u$ is small, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{∂x}{∂u}|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})} & ≈\\frac{x\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})-x\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0})}{\\delta u} \\\\ {\\frac{∂y}{∂u}|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})} & ≈\\frac{y\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})-y\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0})}{\\delta u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector $\\mathbf s_1$ can be approximated as follows:\n\n![](images/b7/b766b50b744af71539daaf12c1e32e44f493ac71bbabb8b387c78bb8092886b8.svg)\n\nUsing similar arguments, we have the following approximation for $\\mathbf s_2{:}$\n\n![](images/f3/f3203536e63ab67521014fe9e8d623cf00535ae8d35adee9b6405253d532dcfc.svg)\n\nThe area of $D$ is approximately equal to the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\mathbf s_1$ and $\\mathbf s_2.$ Therefore,\n\n![](images/55/55a8a36b0617400f963430eb1ba31ef20cbddeb4f09eaeaa8920fae39b4b145e.svg)\n\nSubstituting our approximations for $\\mathbf s_1$ and $\\mathbf s_2,$ we arrive at the desired result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ≈{|det[\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\delta u\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\,\\,\\delta v\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂v}\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\delta u\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\delta v\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\,\\,\\end{aligned}]|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})} \\\\  & ={|\\delta u\\delta v\\cdot (\\frac{∂x}{∂u}\\frac{∂y}{∂v}-\\frac{∂x}{∂v}\\frac{∂y}{∂u})|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})} \\\\  & =\\delta u\\delta v\\cdot {|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535472 17592186045443 false] [17592186158609 1064 "We'll now justify why the Jacobian determinant gives the (local) area scale factor of a transformation.\n\nConsider a rectangular region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane of length $\\delta u,$ width $\\delta v,$ whose bottom-left corner is at the point $(u_0,v_0),$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/9a/9a7d700c343fd2807e70b43d688c2f47ed3724e88fc9849e23aa20ee0be10b15.png)\n\nFurthermore, we assume the following:\n\n- $\\mathbf T: \\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow \\mathbb R^2$ is a $C^1$ transformation throughout $\\Delta,$ and contains no critical points.\n\n- The image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ is the region $D.$\n\n- The image of $(u_0,v_0)$ is $(x_0,y_0).$\n\n- The image of $S_1$ is $S_1',$ and its endpoints are $(x_0,y_0)$ and $A.$\n\n- The image of $S_2$ is $S_2',$ and its endpoints are $(x_0,y_0)$ and $B.$\n\nSince $S_1'$ is the image of $S_1,$ it can be parametrized by varying $u$ only. Similarly, since $S_2'$ is the image of $S_2,$ it can be parametrized by varying $v$ only. So, the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively, are given by\n\n![](images/1b/1be386cd85ad48bcc75b4d292ebeeee0d8af0bf8df2297696b9384c448130bb3.svg)\n\nNow, since $\\delta u$ is small, $S_1'$ can be approximated by a line segment. Similarly, since $\\d