we construct the matrices $U$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/25/252867b0bccd95be918f05320ee65c7153f499b15ccc77624be18128d0f0de09.svg)" 13194139535472 17592186045443 true] [17592186161816 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA = A^T\n$$\n\nEquivalently, $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+{A}^{T})^{T} & ={A}^{T}+({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}+A \\\\  & =A+{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the sum or difference between two symmetric matrices is symmetric.\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n![](images/81/812e9888b920e0338fd72116464b8b96c8c272f95f180cd46aa3f1db9afac8d2.svg)\nTheir product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535472 17592186045443 false] [17592186161816 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA = A^T\n$$\n\nEquivalently, $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+{A}^{T})^{T} & ={A}^{T}+({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}+A \\\\  & =A+{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the sum or difference between two symmetric matrices is symmetric.\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n![](images/81/812e9888b920e0338fd72116464b8b96c8c272f95f180cd46aa3f1db9afac8d2.svg)\nTheir product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535472 17592186045443 true] [17592186161835 1074 "Recall that if  $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3 \\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $\\Sigma$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3 \\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2 \\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}26 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 26\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values. From the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that $\\sigma_1=6$ and $\\sigma_2=4.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T \\! A$):\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=\\sigma_1^2=36,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-36I & =[\\begin{aligned}26 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 26\\end{aligned}]-36[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-10 & -10 \\\\ -10 & -10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A^T \\! A - 36I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the unit eigenvector\n![](images/d7/d7fa1eea4f89ef21e80d7e090d4487447cfc7a78b6edc99df4b3fd4fe51ab03a.svg)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=\\sigma_2^2=16,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-16I & =[\\begin{aligned}26 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 26\\end{aligned}]-16[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}10 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A^T \\! A - 16I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the unit eigenvector\n![](images/2c/2cc8e66369af5363ecfe712442910d939308452a61405e77c311bb3bc993bbae.svg)\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/61/6149abf5eabb91a50f0bee958e2a1fbaf547b75f10437f5449d8e06ea0789153.svg)" 13194139535472 17592186045443 false] [17592186161835 1074 "Recall that if  $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3 \\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $\\Sigma$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3 \\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2 \\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}26 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 26\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values. From the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that $\\sigma_1=6$ and $\\sigma_2=4.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T \\! A$):\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=\\sigma_1^2=36,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-36I & =\\begin{bmatrix}26 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 26\\end{bmatrix}-36\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & -10 \\\\ -10 & -10\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A^T \\! A - 36I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the unit eigenvector\n![](images/d7/d7fa1eea4f89ef21e80d7e090d4487447cfc7a78b6edc99df4b3fd4fe51ab03a.svg)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=\\sigma_2^2=16,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-16I & =\\begin{bmatrix}26 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 26\\end{bmatrix}-16\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A^T \\! A - 16I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the unit eigenvector\n![](images/2c/2cc8e66369af5363ecfe712442910d939308452a61405e77c311bb3bc993bbae.svg)\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/61/6149abf5eabb91a50f0bee958e2a1fbaf547b75f10437f5449d8e06ea0789153.svg)" 13194139535472 17592186045443 true] [17592186161836 1074 "Recall that if  $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3 \\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $\\Sigma$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3 \\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2 \\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that $\\sigma_1=\\sqrt5$ and $\\sigma_2=0.$ \nAlso, the columns $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the $1$st column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the second singular value is $\\sigma_2=0$, and we do not have a third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n![](images/e8/e86a2c3e4c978a2dc3e5baf3b5ff756cf8274e4927b48728372525d0f4167dab.svg)\n\nsuch that $\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = 0$ and $\\|\\mathbf{u}\\|=1.$\n\nSolving